# Klatchly behind Caddy on the host (example). Replace klatchly.example.com with your domain (= KLATCHLY_ORIGIN / KLATCHLY_RP_ID in
# .env) and 8080 with KLATCHLY_PORT. Caddy obtains the certificate itself (automatic HTTPS).
# - Auth is the app's (passkeys): an unauthenticated request gets 200 (the SPA) or a 302 to /, never 401/403; a
#   refused WebSocket gets 400. The device API under /api/bearer/ (the share package's iOS Shortcut, with a token)
#   answers 400 bad_token, 404, 413 or 429, never 401/403 either.
# - flush_interval -1: the WebSocket (/api/ws) and streamed media (Range) must not be buffered.
# - The log masks the url parameter of /api/media (a media identifier), and the title, text and url of a link shared
#   into /share (Android's share sheet opens /share?title=&text=&url=: what the user shares stays out of the log).
# - Uploads: the app caps /api/upload at 100 MB; Caddy cuts at 101 MB. If you put a web application firewall in
#   front (for example a CrowdSec appsec `import`), exclude /api/upload from it: such firewalls often reject bodies
#   over a few MB. Do not buffer /api/ws or /api/media either.
# - Set trusted proxies in Klatchly: Caddy reaches the container from the compose network's gateway
#   (KLATCHLY_TRUSTED_PROXIES in .env).
klatchly.example.com {
	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/klatchly.access.log
		format filter {
			fields {
				request>uri query {
					replace url REDACTED
					replace text REDACTED
					replace title REDACTED
				}
			}
		}
	}
	header -Server
	@upload path /api/upload
	handle @upload {
		request_body {
			max_size 101MB
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080 {
			flush_interval -1
		}
	}
}
